Source: sp9-dkms
Section: kernel
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), dh-dkms
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://github.com/joonhoekim/surface-pro-9-linux
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: sp9-dkms
Architecture: all
Depends: dkms (>= 3.0), linux-headers-surface, kmod, libelf1t64 | libelf1, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: mokutil
Description: Surface Pro 9 kernel patches as DKMS modules for the linux-surface kernel
 Rebuilds eleven modules of the linux-surface kernel with the kernel patches of
 surface-pro-9-linux and installs them to /lib/modules/<release>/updates/dkms,
 where they take precedence over the stock ones: front and rear cameras
 (ipu-bridge, ov5693, ov13858, dw9719, intel_skl_int3472_discrete), USB-C
 (ucsi_surface_hid, new) and the charger (surface_charger), SAM RTC node and
 fan floor (surface_aggregator_registry, surface_platform_profile), Type Cover
 keys and backlight (hid-surface) and touch (ithc).
 .
 Each module is generated from the exact source of each supported
 linux-surface release, so no stable or linux-surface fix of that release is
 replaced by an older copy. For a kernel release the package has no source
 for, nothing is built and the stock modules stay in use; sp9-dkms-status
 says which kernels are covered. With Secure Boot, dkms signs the modules with
 its own key, which has to be enrolled once with mokutil.
 .
 The display patch (PSR2 quirk) and the Bluetooth patches are not part of this
 package; Bluetooth is in sp9-dkms-bluetooth.

Package: sp9-dkms-bluetooth
Architecture: all
Depends: dkms (>= 3.0), linux-headers-surface, kmod, libelf1t64 | libelf1, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: sp9-dkms
Description: Surface Pro 9 Bluetooth patches as a DKMS bluetooth.ko for linux-surface
 Rebuilds the whole Bluetooth core module (bluetooth.ko) with two patches: LE
 legacy pairing with out-of-band data, which the Slim Pen 2 uses when it is
 bonded in the Type Cover's pen slot, and replacing a controller resolving
 list entry when a device is bonded again, without which a re-bonded pen does
 not reconnect until the adapter is restarted.
 .
 Trade-off: bluetooth.ko is large, security-relevant and fixed often in stable
 releases. To never hide those fixes, the module is built only from the
 net/bluetooth source of exactly the running kernel release, which this
 package carries for each supported linux-surface release. For any other
 release nothing is built and the stock bluetooth.ko is used (sp9-dkms-status
 says so), so after a kernel update the pen features are missing until this
 package is updated. While installed, every Bluetooth device on the system
 uses this rebuilt module. Skip this package if you do not need the pen's
 cover coupling.
